[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 803,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 607,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123037,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 607,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intermixed ganglioneuroblastoma (GNBi) is a form of peripheral neuroblastic tumour transitional between neuroblastoma and ganglioneuroma, composed of Schwannian stroma containing scattered well-defined foci of neuroblastic cells at various stages of maturation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 804,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 608,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123038,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 608,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9490/3 Ganglioneuroblastoma, intermixed</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 805,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 609,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123039,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 609,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2A00.21 &amp; XH0RB7 Mixed neuronal-glial tumours &amp; Ganglioneuroblastoma, intermixed</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 806,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 610,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123040,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 610,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> Schwannian stroma&ndash;rich intermixed tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 807,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 611,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123041,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 611,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 808,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 612,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123042,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 612,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GNBi occurs most frequently in the abdominal cavity&nbsp;/ retroperitoneum (usually adrenal), but also in the mediastinum, cervical region, and pelvis {28465480}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 809,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 613,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123043,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 613,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GNBi often presents as an asymptomatic mass {27465021; 29369484; 19530234}, generally lacking the elevated urine catecholamines seen in neuroblastoma. Imaging shows an encapsulated, well-circumscribed mass. MRI diffusion-weighting characteristics may help distinguish ganglioneuroma/GNBi from neuroblastoma {20462716; 27340927}. Tumours are less likely to be MIBG-avid than neuroblastomas {27465021; 29369484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 810,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 614,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123044,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 614,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ganglioneuroma and GNBi together account for approximately 25% of peripheral neuroblastic tumours {27465021; 29369484; 19530234}. Compared with neuroblastoma, GNBi tumours occur in older children (median age: 4&ndash;7&nbsp;years) and patients are less likely to have elevated urine catecholamines {27465021; 29369484}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 811,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 615,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123045,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 615,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 812,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 616,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123046,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 616,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Peripheral neuroblastic tumours originate from neural crest cells that give rise to adrenal medulla and sympathetic ganglia.</p>\r\n<p>Histologically and genetically low-risk neuroblastomas (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/53/102\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Neuroblastoma</em>) may spontaneously mature into GNBi {10421272; 8618605}. <em>MYCN</em> oncogene amplification is rare (&lt;&nbsp;1%) {19530234; 22744966}. Segmental chromosomal aberrations are infrequent, but neuroblastic cells may harbour numerical chromosomal aberrations. DNA content is frequently diploid {19530234} because of an abundance of stromal cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 819,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 623,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123047,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 623,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>GNBi is a solid, well-defined mass displaying a homogeneous pale-grey cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 813,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 617,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123048,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 617,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Well-circumscribed lacunar areas of neuroblastic cells embedded in neuropil are randomly distributed in ganglioneuromatous tissue composed of Schwannian cells and maturing/mature ganglionic cells. The neuroblastic cells comprise various stages of differentiation. No pushing borders are encountered between areas of neuroblastic cells and the ganglioneuromatous component {10421272}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 814,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 618,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123049,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 618,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears and touch imprints show a heterogeneous population of larger ganglionic cells with large round to mildly irregular nuclei; prominent single nucleoli; considerable coarsely granular, dark blue (Giemsa), usually eccentric cytoplasm; a variable number of smaller neuroblasts with the same features as those seen in neuroblastoma; and a variable amount of Schwannian stroma {31231909}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 815,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 619,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123050,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 619,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 818,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 622,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123051,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 622,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a Schwannian stroma&ndash;prominent tumour; well-demarcated microscopic foci of neuropil and mainly differentiating neuroblastic cells; a ganglioneuromatous component constituting &gt;&nbsp;50% of the tumour volume.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunohistochemistry for synaptophysin and S100 aids in distinguishing neuropil and Schwann cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 816,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 620,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123052,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 620,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>All neuroblastic tumours are staged according to the International Neuroblastoma Risk Group Staging System (INRGSS) {19047290}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 817,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 621,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123053,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 621,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>According to the INRGSS, GNBi is usually a locoregional tumour, classified as low risk with extremely rare metastatic spread {27465021; 29369484; 20462716}. GNBi is usually chemotherapy-insensitive {27465021}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T12:54:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]